From 414a5bc8cf76382879d1d901800b57bd13be0fc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Asay <aasay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:15:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Join existing namespaces by path: ns:<path> for network, UTS
 and IPC

Accept Podman's ns:<absolute path> form for HostConfig.NetworkMode,
UTSMode and IpcMode, so a container can join namespaces that something
outside Kiri created and pinned (for example with `ip netns add`). Several
containers given the same paths share one network, hostname and IPC
namespace, the way Podman pod members do, without an infra container.

The namespaces are used as they are: Kiri makes and removes nothing in
them. With ns: networking there is no bridge, veth, address, published
port or extra network, and port publishing, a MAC address and links are
refused. The container keeps its own /etc/hosts and resolv.conf, so
--hostname, --dns and --add-host still apply when UTS is not joined.

In a joined UTS namespace the hostname is the namespace's: the child sets
no hostname or domain name, and $HOSTNAME, /etc/hostname and /etc/hosts
use the hostname the agent reads from the namespace. Hostname and
Domainname from the client are ignored rather than refused (Podman
refuses them), so Docker clients that always send them keep working.
The IPC namespace is joined before the mounts, so /dev/mqueue belongs to
it.

The daemon checks each path is absolute and passes it to the agent as a
new net option (netns_path, utsns_path, ipcns_path). The agent refuses
anything that is not a regular file before opening it (a FIFO would block
it, a device would run its driver), opens it non-blocking, checks its type
with NS_GET_NSTYPE and joins it in the child instead of making a new
namespace. Other modes behave as before. inspect shows the
modes, and docker exec and restart land in the same namespaces.
